# Undefined behavior

Detect the following UB:

## Mutation during iteration

The language reference explicitly says behavior is undefined. CPython raises RuntimeError for dict and set, but the language does not guarantee even that. This is genuine UB in the C sense — different implementations can do different things and none is wrong.

## `__hash__` / `__eq__` inconsistency

Violate a == b ⇒ hash(a) == hash(b) and set/dict membership becomes logically undefined — not "raises an exception", but "returns whatever falls out of the bucket layout".

## Concurrent access without synchronization

A race on `list.append` happens to be atomic-under-GIL in current CPython, but the language doesn't promise this, and PEP 703 (free-threaded CPython) is busy removing the de-facto guarantee. Cross-implementation, races are full UB.

## C extension boundary

ctypes, the buffer protocol, raw pointers from numpy — once you cross into C, you inherit all of C's UB.

## `__del__` ordering and finalizer resurrection

When finalizers run, what state they see, and whether they run on interpreter shutdown is implementation-defined to the point of being effectively undefined.

